  Question
 One of the DevOps engineers was trying to deploy a python app on Kubernetes cluster. Unfortunately, due to some mis-configuration, the application is not coming up. Please take a look into it and fix the issues. Application should be accessible on the specified nodePort.
The deployment name is python-deployment-datacenter, its using poroko/flask-demo-app image. The deployment and service of this app is already deployed.
nodePort should be 32345 and targetPort should be python flask app's default port.
Note: The kubectl utility on the jump-host has been configured to work with the Kubernetes cluster.

          Solution
1. Create a deployment
    vi deployment.yaml
Paste the manifest
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: python-deployment-datacenter
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: python-datacenter
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: python-datacenter
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: python-container
          image: poroko/flask-demo-app
          ports:
            - containerPort: 5000
-Apply it
     kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
2. Fix the service
     vi service.yaml
Paste the manifest
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: python-service-datacenter
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: python-datacenter
  ports:
    - port: 5000
      targetPort: 5000
      nodePort: 32345
-Apply it
    kubectl apply -f service.yaml
3. Validate
-Check pods:
   kubectl get pods -l app=python-datacenter
   kubectl logs <pod-name>
-Check service
    kubectl get svc python-service-datacenter

